export default "# Cloud Networking Fundamentals: Security Groups and Firewalls\n\n# PART 1 OF 4: FOUNDATIONS OF NETWORK SECURITY\n\n## 1. Introduction to Network Security\n\nIn cloud computing, applications communicate through networks.\n\nFor example, consider an online marketplace:\n\n- Customers access the website through a browser.\n- The frontend communicates with a backend API.\n- The backend API communicates with a database.\n- Background workers process orders and notifications.\n\nEach component communicates through network connections.\n\nHowever, not every connection should be allowed.\n\nImagine that your production database accepts connections from every computer on the internet. An attacker could discover the database and attempt to access it.\n\nThis is why cloud environments use network security controls.\n\n**Network security controls** are mechanisms that determine which network traffic is allowed or denied.\n\nThey help protect:\n- Virtual machines.\n- Databases.\n- APIs.\n- Application servers.\n- Internal networks.\n- Cloud storage services.\n- Containers and Kubernetes workloads.\n\nTwo important network security mechanisms are:\n\n1. Security groups.\n2. Firewalls.\n\n## 2. What Is a Firewall?\n\nA firewall is a security mechanism that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic according to defined rules.\n\nA firewall decides whether traffic should be:\n\n- Allowed.\n- Blocked.\n- Rejected.\n- Logged or inspected, depending on the firewall.\n\nFor example, imagine a Linux server running an API.\n\nYou want:\n- HTTP traffic on port 80 to be allowed.\n- HTTPS traffic on port 443 to be allowed.\n- SSH traffic on port 22 to be allowed only from your administrator's IP address.\n- Other unnecessary incoming connections to be blocked.\n\nA firewall can enforce these restrictions.\n\n### Real-world example\n\nSuppose your server has this public IP address:\n\n`203.0.113.10`\n\nYou configure firewall rules:\n\n| Port | Protocol | Purpose | Access |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 22 | TCP | SSH administration | Administrator IP only |\n| 80 | TCP | HTTP | Public |\n| 443 | TCP | HTTPS | Public |\n| 3306 | TCP | MySQL database | Blocked publicly |\n| 27017 | TCP | MongoDB | Blocked publicly |\n\nThis configuration reduces the number of services exposed to the internet.\n\n**Important:** A firewall does not automatically make an application secure. Authentication, authorization, encryption, secure coding, and patching are also necessary.\n\n## 3. Understanding Network Traffic\n\nBefore configuring firewalls, understand what a network connection contains.\n\nA network connection involves several important elements.\n\n| Element | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Source IP | Where traffic originates |\n| Destination IP | Where traffic is going |\n| Source port | Port used by the sending application |\n| Destination port | Port where the receiving service listens |\n| Protocol | Communication method, such as TCP or UDP |\n| Direction | Incoming or outgoing traffic |\n\n### Example: Connecting to a web server\n\nA customer opens:\n\n`https://marketplace.example.com`\n\nThe browser establishes a connection to the web server.\n\nA simplified representation:\n\n```text\nCustomer Browser\n      |\n      | TCP connection\n      | Destination port: 443\n      v\nCloud Firewall\n      |\n      | Checks security rules\n      v\nWeb Server\n      |\n      v\nWebsite\n```\n\nThe firewall checks whether the connection is permitted.\n\nIf HTTPS traffic on port 443 is allowed, the connection can proceed.\n\nIf the relevant traffic is blocked, the connection fails.\n\n## 4. Understanding TCP and UDP\n\nFirewalls frequently use network protocols when defining rules.\n\n### TCP — Transmission Control Protocol\n\nTCP establishes a connection between communicating systems.\n\nIt provides:\n- Reliable data delivery.\n- Ordered data transmission.\n- Error detection and retransmission.\n- Connection management.\n\nCommon TCP applications include:\n\n| Port | Service |\n|---|---|\n| 22 | SSH |\n| 25 | SMTP |\n| 53 | DNS over TCP |\n| 80 | HTTP |\n| 443 | HTTPS |\n| 3306 | MySQL |\n| 5432 | PostgreSQL |\n| 27017 | MongoDB |\n\n### UDP — User Datagram Protocol\n\nUDP sends datagrams without establishing a connection in the same way TCP does.\n\nIt generally has lower protocol overhead but does not guarantee delivery or ordering.\n\nCommon UDP applications include:\n\n| Port | Service |\n|---|---|\n| 53 | DNS |\n| 67/68 | DHCP |\n| 123 | NTP |\n| 161 | SNMP |\n\nSome applications use both TCP and UDP, depending on their requirements.\n\n### Why does this matter?\n\nA firewall rule must identify the correct protocol.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nAllow TCP port 443\n```\n\nThis allows HTTPS traffic using TCP.\n\nIt does not automatically allow UDP traffic on port 443, which may be used by HTTP/3 over QUIC.\n\n## 5. What Is a Security Group?\n\nA security group is a cloud networking security control that defines which traffic can reach or leave supported cloud resources.\n\nSecurity groups are commonly associated with resources such as:\n\n- Virtual machines.\n- Cloud network interfaces.\n- Database instances.\n- Other supported cloud resources.\n\nSecurity groups are commonly described as virtual firewalls.\n\nFor example, in AWS, a security group can be attached to an EC2 instance or network interface.\n\nIn Azure, Network Security Groups (NSGs) control traffic to and from supported network interfaces and subnets.\n\nIn Google Cloud, VPC firewall rules control traffic to and from eligible network resources.\n\nThe implementations differ, so these terms should not be treated as identical products.\n\n### Example security group\n\nImagine an application server.\n\nYou configure the following inbound rules:\n\n| Protocol | Port | Source | Action |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| TCP | 443 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Allow |\n| TCP | 22 | `198.51.100.10/32` | Allow |\n| TCP | 3306 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Deny or leave unallowed |\n\nThe application accepts HTTPS from the internet and SSH from one administrator address.\n\nThe database port is not publicly exposed.\n\n**CIDR reminder:** `198.51.100.10/32` identifies one IPv4 address. `0.0.0.0/0` represents all IPv4 addresses.\n\nThese example IP ranges are reserved for documentation and are not real public customer addresses.\n\n## 6. Security Groups Versus Traditional Firewalls\n\nAlthough both protect network traffic, they are not always implemented in the same way.\n\n| Feature | Security Group | Traditional/Host Firewall |\n|---|---|---|\n| Location | Cloud infrastructure | Server or network device |\n| Main purpose | Control resource network access | Control network traffic |\n| Configuration | Cloud console, API or IaC | OS tools or firewall appliance |\n| Examples | AWS Security Groups | Linux UFW, Windows Firewall |\n| Scope | Cloud resource or interface | Host or network boundary |\n\nA cloud environment can use both.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nInternet\n   |\n   v\nCloud Security Group\n   |\n   v\nLinux Server\n   |\n   v\nUFW Firewall\n   |\n   v\nApplication\n```\n\nThe cloud security group controls traffic before it reaches the server's operating system.\n\nUFW provides another layer of protection inside the Linux server.\n\n## 7. Inbound and Outbound Rules\n\nFirewall rules commonly control two traffic directions.\n\n### Inbound traffic\n\nInbound traffic is traffic entering a protected resource.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nInternet\n   |\n   | HTTPS request\n   v\nApplication Server\n```\n\nA firewall rule might allow TCP port 443 from all IPv4 addresses.\n\n### Outbound traffic\n\nOutbound traffic is traffic leaving a protected resource.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nApplication Server\n   |\n   | HTTPS request\n   v\nExternal Payment API\n```\n\nAn outbound rule can restrict which destinations and ports the server may access.\n\n### Why restrict outbound traffic?\n\nSuppose an attacker compromises your application server.\n\nIf outbound traffic is unrestricted, the attacker might attempt to:\n- Communicate with external command-and-control infrastructure.\n- Exfiltrate sensitive data.\n- Download malicious files.\n- Scan external systems.\n\nRestricting outbound traffic can reduce the damage caused by a compromised server.\n\nHowever, outbound restrictions must be designed carefully because applications depend on DNS, software updates, APIs, monitoring, and other services.\n\n## 8. Default Security Group Behaviour\n\nSecurity group defaults differ by cloud provider.\n\nFor example, AWS security groups are stateful and traditionally have:\n- No inbound traffic allowed by default when a new security group is created.\n- All outbound IPv4 traffic allowed by default in the default outbound configuration.\n\nAzure NSGs and Google Cloud firewall rules have different default and priority behaviours.\n\nNever assume that every cloud provider uses the same defaults.\n\nAlways inspect the actual rules attached to the resource.\n\n## 9. Stateful and Stateless Firewalls\n\nThis is one of the most important networking concepts.\n\n### Stateful firewall\n\nA stateful firewall tracks active connections.\n\nSuppose a customer connects to your server:\n\n```text\nCustomer\n   |\n   | TCP request\n   v\nWeb Server\n```\n\nIf the firewall allows the incoming connection, it can recognize the returning traffic as belonging to that established connection.\n\nYou generally do not need to create a separate inbound rule for every response packet.\n\nAWS security groups are stateful.\n\n### Stateless firewall\n\nA stateless firewall evaluates packets independently against its rules.\n\nIt does not automatically remember the connection state in the same way.\n\nYou may need to allow both the original traffic and the corresponding return traffic.\n\nFor example, AWS Network ACLs are stateless.\n\n### Comparison\n\n| Feature | Stateful | Stateless |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tracks connections | Yes | No |\n| Understands established sessions | Yes | No connection tracking |\n| Return traffic | Automatically permitted when associated with an allowed connection, subject to the implementation | Must be permitted by applicable rules |\n| Example | AWS Security Groups | AWS Network ACLs |\n\n**Important:** Stateful does not mean all return traffic is automatically permitted under every possible condition. Connection tracking, rule changes, and platform-specific behaviour still matter.\n\n## 10. The Principle of Least Privilege\n\nThe principle of least privilege means giving a system or user only the access necessary to perform its responsibilities.\n\nFor network security, this means allowing only required connections.\n\nConsider these two configurations.\n\n**Configuration A:**\n\n```text\nAllow all inbound traffic\nAllow all outbound traffic\n```\n\n**Configuration B:**\n\n```text\nAllow HTTPS from the internet\nAllow SSH from administrator IP\nAllow database access from application servers\nBlock unnecessary inbound traffic\n```\n\nConfiguration B is safer because it limits exposure.\n\nThis principle should guide every firewall decision.\n\n## 11. Real-World Architecture\n\nConsider a cloud marketplace.\n\nIt has:\n\n- A frontend.\n- A backend API.\n- A database.\n- A background worker.\n\nA secure network design could look like this:\n\n```text\n                  INTERNET\n                      |\n                      v\n               WEB SECURITY GROUP\n                      |\n                      v\n                FRONTEND SERVER\n                      |\n                      v\n                API SECURITY GROUP\n                      |\n                      v\n                  BACKEND API\n                      |\n                      v\n              DATABASE SECURITY GROUP\n                      |\n                      v\n                 DATABASE SERVER\n```\n\nExample access policy:\n\n| Resource | Allowed inbound traffic |\n|---|---|\n| Frontend | HTTPS from internet |\n| Backend API | Application traffic from frontend or approved entry point |\n| Database | Database connections from backend only |\n| Worker | Required internal application and queue connections |\n\nThe database should not accept public connections merely because the frontend is publicly accessible.\n\n## Part 1 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- What network security controls do.\n- What a firewall is.\n- How network traffic is evaluated.\n- TCP and UDP.\n- Security groups.\n- Inbound and outbound rules.\n- Stateful and stateless firewalls.\n- Least privilege.\n- How to protect a cloud marketplace architecture.\n\n**Progress: Part 1 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 OF 4: ADVANCED FIREWALL DESIGN AND CLOUD IMPLEMENTATIONS\n\n## 1. Understanding Firewall Rule Components\n\nA firewall rule is a policy that determines whether specific traffic is allowed or denied.\n\nA typical rule contains:\n\n| Component | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Direction | Inbound |\n| Protocol | TCP |\n| Port | 443 |\n| Source | `0.0.0.0/0` |\n| Destination | Application server |\n| Action | Allow |\n| Description | Public HTTPS access |\n\nConsider this rule:\n\n```text\nDirection: Inbound\nProtocol: TCP\nPort: 443\nSource: 0.0.0.0/0\nAction: Allow\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `Inbound` means traffic is entering the protected resource.\n- `TCP` identifies the transport protocol.\n- `443` is the destination port.\n- `0.0.0.0/0` allows connections from any IPv4 address.\n- `Allow` permits matching traffic.\n\nThe rule makes the HTTPS service publicly reachable, assuming the remaining network configuration also permits access.\n\n## 2. Understanding Port Ranges\n\nA firewall can allow one port or a range of ports.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nTCP 8000\n```\n\nAllows TCP traffic to port 8000.\n\nAnother example:\n\n```text\nTCP 8000-8100\n```\n\nAllows TCP traffic to destination ports 8000 through 8100.\n\nAvoid opening large port ranges unless the application genuinely requires them.\n\nFor example, allowing:\n\n```text\nTCP 1-65535\n```\n\nexposes all TCP destination ports permitted by the other network controls.\n\nThat is rarely an appropriate production configuration.\n\n## 3. Source IP Restrictions\n\nA firewall can restrict access based on source IP addresses.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nAllow TCP port 22\nSource: 198.51.100.25/32\n```\n\nOnly the specified IPv4 address is permitted by this rule.\n\nYou could also permit an administrator network:\n\n```text\n198.51.100.0/24\n```\n\nThis represents 256 IPv4 addresses.\n\nHowever, remember that a broad CIDR range gives access to more potential sources.\n\nUse the smallest practical range.\n\n### Why should SSH not be open to everyone?\n\nA rule like:\n\n```text\nAllow TCP 22 from 0.0.0.0/0\n```\n\nmakes SSH reachable from every IPv4 address, subject to the other network controls.\n\nAttackers routinely scan public IP ranges for exposed SSH services.\n\nA better approach is to:\n\n- Restrict SSH to trusted administrator addresses.\n- Use SSH keys.\n- Disable password authentication where practical.\n- Use a VPN or managed access service.\n- Consider a cloud bastion host or session-management service.\n- Monitor authentication attempts.\n\n## 4. Security Groups in AWS\n\nAWS provides Security Groups for controlling network access to supported resources.\n\nThey are stateful virtual firewalls.\n\n### Example AWS architecture\n\n```text\nInternet\n   |\n   v\nLoad Balancer\n   |\n   v\nWeb Security Group\n   |\n   v\nApplication Security Group\n   |\n   v\nDatabase Security Group\n```\n\nYou could configure:\n\n**Load balancer security group**\n\n| Protocol | Port | Source |\n|---|---:|---|\n| TCP | 443 | Internet |\n\n**Application security group**\n\n| Protocol | Port | Source |\n|---|---:|---|\n| TCP | 3000 | Load balancer security group |\n\n**Database security group**\n\n| Protocol | Port | Source |\n|---|---:|---|\n| TCP | 5432 | Application security group |\n\nThis allows the application to connect to PostgreSQL without exposing the database publicly.\n\n### Referencing another security group\n\nAWS allows security group rules to reference other security groups in supported configurations.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nDatabase inbound:\nTCP 5432\nSource: Application-Security-Group\n```\n\nThis is generally more manageable than maintaining a list of changing application-server IP addresses.\n\nWhen instances are added or replaced, their membership in the referenced security group continues to determine which resources are permitted.\n\n## 5. Security Groups in Microsoft Azure\n\nAzure uses Network Security Groups (NSGs).\n\nAn NSG contains inbound and outbound security rules.\n\nAn NSG can be associated with:\n\n- A subnet.\n- A network interface.\n\nAn NSG rule includes:\n\n- Priority.\n- Source.\n- Source port.\n- Destination.\n- Destination port.\n- Protocol.\n- Action.\n\n### Example Azure NSG rules\n\n| Priority | Direction | Protocol | Port | Source | Action |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|---|\n| 100 | Inbound | TCP | 443 | Internet | Allow |\n| 110 | Inbound | TCP | 22 | Admin network | Allow |\n| 200 | Inbound | Any | Any | Any | Deny |\n\nAzure NSG rules use priority values.\n\nLower numerical priority values are evaluated before higher numerical values.\n\nFor example, priority 100 is evaluated before priority 200.\n\nAzure also provides default security rules. These must be considered when examining the final effective access policy.\n\n### Important Azure concept: Effective security rules\n\nA network interface may be affected by an NSG attached to the interface and another NSG attached to its subnet.\n\nYou must consider both.\n\nA rule that permits traffic at one level does not necessarily mean the complete network path permits it.\n\n## 6. Firewall Rules in Google Cloud\n\nGoogle Cloud provides VPC firewall rules.\n\nThese rules control traffic to and from supported resources in a VPC network.\n\nRules can use:\n\n- Source IP ranges.\n- Destination IP ranges, where applicable.\n- Protocols.\n- Ports.\n- Network tags or service accounts as targets, depending on rule type.\n- Direction and priority.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nDirection: INGRESS\nProtocol: TCP\nPort: 443\nSource: 0.0.0.0/0\nAction: ALLOW\n```\n\nThis permits matching HTTPS traffic to eligible targets.\n\nGoogle Cloud also has hierarchical firewall policies that can apply at broader organizational levels.\n\nTherefore, a VPC firewall rule is not always the only firewall policy affecting a resource.\n\n## 7. Comparing AWS, Azure and Google Cloud\n\n| Feature | AWS | Azure | Google Cloud |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Main control | Security Groups | Network Security Groups | VPC Firewall Rules |\n| Stateful behaviour | Yes | Yes | Stateful connection tracking |\n| Rule priority | Security group rules are not evaluated using a user-defined numerical priority system | Yes | Yes |\n| Inbound control | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Outbound control | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Common scope | Network interface/resource | Subnet or network interface | VPC network and targeted resources |\n\nCloud security services have different capabilities and defaults. Always consult the provider documentation before deploying production rules.\n\n## 8. Network ACLs\n\nA Network Access Control List (Network ACL or NACL) is a network-level control used in AWS VPCs.\n\nUnlike AWS security groups, NACLs are stateless.\n\nThey can control traffic entering and leaving a subnet.\n\n### Security Groups vs Network ACLs\n\n| Feature | Security Group | Network ACL |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope | Resource/network interface | Subnet |\n| Stateful | Yes | No |\n| Allow rules | Yes | Yes |\n| Deny rules | No explicit deny rules | Yes |\n| Rule evaluation | All applicable rules are combined | Numbered rules evaluated in order |\n| Return traffic | Automatically handled for tracked connections | Must be allowed separately |\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose a subnet contains an application server.\n\nYou configure:\n\n**Security group**\n\n```text\nAllow TCP 443 from internet\n```\n\n**Network ACL**\n\n```text\nAllow TCP 443 inbound\nAllow required ephemeral ports outbound\n```\n\nThe NACL must also allow the necessary return traffic because it is stateless.\n\n### What are ephemeral ports?\n\nEphemeral ports are temporary source ports used by clients and operating systems for network connections.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nClient: 192.0.2.10:51524\nServer: 203.0.113.10:443\n```\n\nThe client uses temporary source port `51524` to communicate with server port `443`.\n\nWhen the server responds, the response is sent back to the client's temporary port.\n\nA stateless ACL must permit the relevant return traffic.\n\nThe exact ephemeral port range depends on the operating system and application environment.\n\nDo not blindly open every port without checking the required ranges.\n\n## 9. Host-Based Firewalls\n\nCloud security groups protect resources at the cloud networking layer.\n\nHost-based firewalls operate inside the operating system.\n\nLinux commonly uses tools such as:\n\n- UFW.\n- firewalld.\n- nftables.\n- iptables on systems that use the legacy interface.\n\nWindows Server provides Windows Defender Firewall.\n\nUsing both cloud-level and host-level controls creates defence in depth.\n\n### Defence in depth\n\nDefence in depth means protecting a system with multiple security layers.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nInternet\n   |\n   v\nCloud Security Group\n   |\n   v\nLinux Operating System\n   |\n   v\nUFW Firewall\n   |\n   v\nApplication Service\n```\n\nIf one layer is misconfigured, another may still provide protection.\n\nHowever, both layers must be maintained and tested.\n\n## 10. Understanding UFW\n\nUFW stands for Uncomplicated Firewall.\n\nIt provides a simpler interface for managing Linux firewall rules.\n\nCheck its status:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status\n```\n\nThis command asks UFW to display its current firewall status.\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nStatus: active\n\nTo                         Action      From\n--                         ------      ----\n22/tcp                     ALLOW       198.51.100.25\n443/tcp                    ALLOW       Anywhere\n```\n\nThis indicates:\n\n- The firewall is active.\n- TCP port 22 is allowed from the specified administrator address.\n- TCP port 443 is allowed from anywhere.\n\n### Check available application profiles\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw app list\n```\n\nThis displays application profiles available to UFW.\n\nAn application profile can define a set of ports and protocols for a service.\n\n### Allow HTTPS\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow 443/tcp\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `sudo` runs the command with administrator privileges.\n- `ufw` invokes the firewall management utility.\n- `allow` creates an allow rule.\n- `443/tcp` specifies TCP destination port 443.\n\n### Allow SSH from one trusted IP\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow from 198.51.100.25 to any port 22 proto tcp\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `allow` permits matching traffic.\n- `from 198.51.100.25` identifies the allowed source.\n- `to any` means the rule applies to any local destination address.\n- `port 22` identifies the SSH destination port.\n- `proto tcp` specifies TCP.\n\n**Production warning:** Before enabling or changing UFW over SSH, ensure that your current administrative access is permitted. Otherwise, you could lock yourself out of the server.\n\n### Set default policies\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw default deny incoming\nsudo ufw default allow outgoing\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nFirst command:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw default deny incoming\n```\n\nBlocks incoming connections that are not explicitly allowed by applicable rules.\n\nSecond command:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw default allow outgoing\n```\n\nAllows outgoing connections by default.\n\nThis is a common starting policy, but production environments may require stricter outbound rules.\n\n### Enable UFW\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw enable\n```\n\nActivates the firewall.\n\nOnly do this after confirming that the required administrative and application access rules exist.\n\n### Remove a rule\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw delete allow 443/tcp\n```\n\nThis removes the matching allow rule.\n\nIf several similar rules exist, inspect numbered rules first:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status numbered\n```\n\nThen delete the intended rule using its number.\n\n## 11. firewalld\n\nSome Linux distributions use firewalld as their firewall management service.\n\nCheck its status:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl status firewalld\n```\n\nThis checks whether the firewalld service is running.\n\nList active firewall zones:\n\n```bash\nsudo firewall-cmd --get-active-zones\n```\n\nA zone groups network interfaces and defines their trust level and firewall behaviour.\n\nDisplay rules in the public zone:\n\n```bash\nsudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all\n```\n\nAllow HTTPS permanently:\n\n```bash\nsudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=https --permanent\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `--zone=public` selects the public firewall zone.\n- `--add-service=https` allows the predefined HTTPS service.\n- `--permanent` saves the rule across restarts and reloads.\n\nApply the permanent configuration:\n\n```bash\nsudo firewall-cmd --reload\n```\n\nThis reloads the firewall configuration.\n\nVerify:\n\n```bash\nsudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-services\n```\n\nYou should see `https` among the allowed services if the rule was successfully applied.\n\n## 12. Firewall Policies for a Production Marketplace\n\nImagine you deploy a marketplace using:\n\n- Frontend server.\n- Node.js backend API.\n- MongoDB database.\n- Background worker.\n\nA sensible starting design:\n\n| Component | Port | Permitted source |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Frontend HTTPS | 443 | Internet |\n| Backend API | 3000 | Frontend or approved load balancer |\n| MongoDB | 27017 | Backend and approved worker |\n| SSH | 22 | Administrator access network |\n| Monitoring agent | Provider-specific | Approved monitoring system |\n\nNotice that the database is not exposed to the public internet.\n\nThe API should not be reachable from arbitrary sources if it is designed to be private behind a load balancer or another controlled entry point.\n\n## 13. Common Firewall Design Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Why it is dangerous | Better approach |\n|---|---|---|\n| Allowing every port | Exposes unnecessary services | Allow only required ports |\n| Public database access | Increases attack surface | Restrict to application resources |\n| Public SSH access | Exposes remote administration | Restrict source or use managed access |\n| Forgetting outbound traffic | Applications may fail or communicate with unwanted destinations | Design egress rules |\n| Confusing TCP and UDP | Rules may not match application traffic | Specify the correct protocol |\n| Forgetting stateless return traffic | Connections may fail | Add the required return rules |\n| Disabling a firewall to fix an issue | Removes a security boundary | Diagnose the specific blocked connection |\n| Ignoring default rules | Unexpected access may remain | Inspect effective rules |\n\n## 14. Important Production Security Practices\n\nFor a production cloud environment:\n\n1. Apply least privilege.\n2. Avoid publicly exposing databases.\n3. Restrict administrative access.\n4. Use private subnets for internal services where appropriate.\n5. Review inbound and outbound policies.\n6. Monitor firewall changes.\n7. Document why every rule exists.\n8. Remove obsolete rules.\n9. Test connectivity after configuration changes.\n10. Use infrastructure as code to make firewall configuration repeatable.\n\n## Part 2 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- Firewall rule components.\n- Port ranges and source restrictions.\n- AWS Security Groups.\n- Azure NSGs.\n- Google Cloud VPC firewall rules.\n- Network ACLs.\n- Stateful and stateless behaviour.\n- UFW and firewalld.\n- Defence in depth.\n- Production firewall design.\n\n**Progress: Part 2 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 OF 4: PRACTICAL FIREWALL CONFIGURATION AND TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nThis section turns the concepts into practical skills.\n\nWe will work with Linux firewall commands, network testing, and a production-style marketplace architecture.\n\n## 1. Practical Environment\n\nAssume you have three Linux servers:\n\n| Server | Private IP | Service |\n|---|---|---|\n| Frontend | 10.0.1.10 | HTTPS |\n| Backend API | 10.0.2.10 | Node.js on port 3000 |\n| Database | 10.0.3.10 | MongoDB on port 27017 |\n\nThe desired communication is:\n\n```text\n                 INTERNET\n                     |\n                     v\n              FRONTEND SERVER\n                10.0.1.10\n                     |\n                     | TCP 3000\n                     v\n               BACKEND API\n                10.0.2.10\n                     |\n                     | TCP 27017\n                     v\n               MONGODB SERVER\n                10.0.3.10\n```\n\nSecurity requirements:\n\n- Public users can access the frontend over HTTPS.\n- Only the frontend or approved entry point can access the backend API.\n- Only the backend can access MongoDB.\n- SSH is restricted to administrator access.\n\nWe will use these addresses as documentation examples. Do not apply them blindly to your own servers.\n\n## 2. Inspect the Linux Network Interfaces\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nip addr\n```\n\nThis displays network interfaces and their assigned IP addresses.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n2: eth0:\n    inet 10.0.2.10/24\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `eth0` is the network interface name in this example.\n- `inet` identifies an IPv4 address.\n- `10.0.2.10/24` is the assigned IP and network prefix.\n\nThis helps verify that the server has the expected address.\n\nCheck the routing table:\n\n```bash\nip route\n```\n\nThis displays the routes the server uses to reach different networks.\n\nA missing or incorrect route can cause connectivity failures even when firewall rules are correct.\n\n## 3. Inspect Listening Services\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nsudo ss -tulnp\n```\n\nThis command displays listening network sockets.\n\nMeaning of the options:\n\n| Option | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `-t` | TCP sockets |\n| `-u` | UDP sockets |\n| `-l` | Listening sockets |\n| `-n` | Numeric addresses and ports |\n| `-p` | Associated processes |\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\ntcp LISTEN 0 511 0.0.0.0:3000\n```\n\nThis indicates a TCP service listening on port 3000 on all IPv4 interfaces.\n\nIf you see:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1:3000\n```\n\nThe service is listening only on the local loopback interface.\n\nThat means another machine cannot normally connect to it using the server's private IP.\n\nThis is a common cause of backend connectivity problems.\n\n## 4. Configure a Linux Firewall with UFW\n\nWe will configure a test server.\n\n**Safety:** Perform these commands on a test VM or through a console that remains accessible if SSH is interrupted. Do not activate a firewall remotely without first allowing your actual SSH source.\n\n### Step 1: Install UFW\n\nOn Ubuntu or Debian-based systems:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install ufw\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `apt update` refreshes package information.\n- `apt install ufw` installs the firewall utility.\n\n### Step 2: Inspect current rules\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status verbose\n```\n\nThis shows whether UFW is active and displays its policies.\n\n### Step 3: Set incoming and outgoing defaults\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw default deny incoming\nsudo ufw default allow outgoing\n```\n\nThe first command blocks unsolicited incoming connections by default.\n\nThe second permits outgoing connections by default.\n\n### Step 4: Permit administrator SSH\n\nFor this example:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow from 198.51.100.25 to any port 22 proto tcp\n```\n\nThis allows TCP SSH traffic from the administrator's example IP address.\n\nReplace the documentation address with the actual trusted source before using this in a real environment.\n\nIf your administrator uses a VPN or a changing IP, design the access policy around that arrangement.\n\n### Step 5: Permit HTTPS\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow 443/tcp\n```\n\nThis permits incoming TCP connections to port 443.\n\n### Step 6: Enable the firewall\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw enable\n```\n\nIf prompted, confirm activation.\n\n### Step 7: Verify\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status numbered\n```\n\nYou should see the rules you created.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nStatus: active\n\n[1] 22/tcp ALLOW IN 198.51.100.25\n[2] 443/tcp ALLOW IN Anywhere\n```\n\nThe exact display may vary by UFW version.\n\n## 5. Configure Access Between the Backend and Database\n\nSuppose MongoDB listens on:\n\n```text\n10.0.3.10:27017\n```\n\nOnly the backend server should connect to it.\n\nOn the database server, configure UFW:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow from 10.0.2.10 to any port 27017 proto tcp\n```\n\nLine-by-line explanation:\n\n- `sudo` gives the command administrative privileges.\n- `ufw` manages the firewall.\n- `allow` permits matching traffic.\n- `from 10.0.2.10` identifies the backend server.\n- `to any` means any local destination address.\n- `port 27017` identifies MongoDB's destination port.\n- `proto tcp` specifies TCP.\n\nThis is more restrictive than:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow 27017/tcp\n```\n\nThe second command allows the port from any source permitted by the remaining network controls.\n\n**Important:** UFW rules are only one layer. The cloud security group, routing, MongoDB bind address, and database authentication must also be configured correctly.\n\n## 6. Test Connectivity with Netcat\n\nNetcat is useful for testing whether a remote TCP port can be reached.\n\nFrom the backend server, run:\n\n```bash\nnc -vz 10.0.3.10 27017\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `nc` starts Netcat.\n- `-v` enables verbose output.\n- `-z` checks the port without sending application data.\n- `10.0.3.10` is the database IP.\n- `27017` is the destination port.\n\nA successful test might display:\n\n```text\nConnection to 10.0.3.10 27017 port [tcp/*] succeeded!\n```\n\nThis indicates that a TCP connection could be established.\n\nIt does not prove that MongoDB authentication works or that database queries will succeed.\n\nIf the connection fails, investigate:\n- Cloud security group rules.\n- Linux firewall rules.\n- Routing.\n- Service listening address.\n- Whether MongoDB is running.\n- Whether the destination IP is correct.\n\n## 7. Test HTTP and HTTPS Access\n\nUse curl:\n\n```bash\ncurl -I https://example.com\n```\n\nThis requests HTTP response headers from the specified HTTPS website.\n\nThe `-I` option requests headers without downloading the full response body.\n\nAn example response:\n\n```text\nHTTP/2 200\ncontent-type: text/html\n```\n\nA successful HTTP status indicates that the server responded to the request.\n\nHowever, a `200` response does not prove that the complete application is secure.\n\n## 8. Inspect UFW Logs\n\nFirewall logs can help identify blocked traffic.\n\nCheck the system journal:\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -k --since \"10 minutes ago\"\n```\n\nThis displays recent kernel log messages.\n\nYou can also inspect the traditional UFW log if logging is enabled:\n\n```bash\nsudo tail -f /var/log/ufw.log\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `tail` displays the end of a file.\n- `-f` continuously follows new log entries.\n- `/var/log/ufw.log` is a common UFW log location when configured.\n\nEnable UFW logging:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw logging medium\n```\n\nThis sets the UFW logging level to medium.\n\nUse firewall logs responsibly. Excessive logging can consume storage and make important events harder to identify.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Scenario: Backend Cannot Reach MongoDB\n\nImagine that your marketplace API returns:\n\n```text\nMongoServerSelectionError:\nServer selection timed out\n```\n\nThe backend cannot establish a successful connection to MongoDB.\n\nDo not immediately disable the firewall.\n\nFollow a systematic troubleshooting process.\n\n### Step 1: Check whether MongoDB is running\n\nOn the database server:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl status mongod\n```\n\nThis checks the MongoDB service status.\n\nIf it is stopped, investigate why before restarting it.\n\n### Step 2: Check the listening port\n\n```bash\nsudo ss -tulnp | grep 27017\n```\n\nThis searches the listening socket output for port 27017.\n\nIf nothing appears, MongoDB may not be listening on that port.\n\n### Step 3: Check the bind address\n\nMongoDB configuration is commonly stored at:\n\n```text\n/etc/mongod.conf\n```\n\nA simplified configuration may contain:\n\n```yaml\nnet:\n  port: 27017\n  bindIp: 127.0.0.1,10.0.3.10\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `net` contains network settings.\n- `port` specifies the listening port.\n- `bindIp` identifies the local IP addresses on which MongoDB listens.\n\nHere, MongoDB listens on loopback and its private address.\n\n**Security warning:** Do not use `0.0.0.0` as a shortcut unless you have a carefully designed network policy and understand the exposure. A private database should normally listen only on the interfaces it needs.\n\n### Step 4: Check the database firewall\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status verbose\n```\n\nConfirm that TCP port 27017 is permitted from the backend server.\n\n### Step 5: Check the cloud security group\n\nVerify that the database's cloud security policy permits:\n\n```text\nProtocol: TCP\nPort: 27017\nSource: Backend security group or backend private IP\n```\n\nDo not use `0.0.0.0/0` for the database source.\n\n### Step 6: Test the connection again\n\nFrom the backend:\n\n```bash\nnc -vz 10.0.3.10 27017\n```\n\nIf the connection succeeds, move on to checking MongoDB authentication and application configuration.\n\nIf it still fails, inspect routes and network ACLs where applicable.\n\n## 10. Troubleshooting Scenario: SSH Access Has Failed\n\nSuppose you cannot connect to your Linux VM:\n\n```bash\nssh ubuntu@203.0.113.10\n```\n\nThe terminal reports:\n\n```text\nConnection timed out\n```\n\nPossible causes include:\n\n| Cause | What to inspect |\n|---|---|\n| Security group blocks SSH | Cloud inbound rules |\n| UFW blocks SSH | `sudo ufw status` |\n| Wrong public IP | Cloud instance information |\n| Server is stopped | VM power state |\n| Incorrect route | `ip route` |\n| SSH service stopped | `systemctl status ssh` or `sshd` |\n| Network ACL blocks traffic | Subnet ACL rules |\n| Incorrect administrator IP | SSH source restriction |\n\nA timeout often suggests that packets are not receiving a response. It does not identify the exact cause.\n\nA different error such as `Connection refused` often indicates that the destination was reached but no service accepted the connection on that port, although firewall behaviour can vary.\n\n## 11. Infrastructure as Code: AWS Security Group Example\n\nCloud networking rules should be repeatable.\n\nInfrastructure as Code (IaC) allows developers and cloud engineers to define infrastructure in configuration files.\n\nHere is a simplified Terraform example that creates an AWS security group for a public HTTPS load balancer.\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"web\" {\n  name        = \"marketplace-web\"\n  description = \"Allow public HTTPS access\"\n  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id\n\n  ingress {\n    description = \"Public HTTPS\"\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = \"tcp\"\n    cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]\n  }\n\n  egress {\n    from_port   = 0\n    to_port     = 0\n    protocol    = \"-1\"\n    cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Explain each section\n\n**Resource declaration**\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"web\" {\n```\n\nCreates an AWS security group resource managed by Terraform.\n\n`aws_security_group` identifies the Terraform resource type.\n\n`web` is the local Terraform name used to reference it.\n\n**Name and description**\n\n```hcl\nname        = \"marketplace-web\"\ndescription = \"Allow public HTTPS access\"\n```\n\nThe first line sets the cloud security group's name.\n\nThe second documents its purpose.\n\n**VPC association**\n\n```hcl\nvpc_id = var.vpc_id\n```\n\nAssociates the security group with the VPC whose ID is supplied through the Terraform variable `vpc_id`.\n\n**Inbound rule**\n\n```hcl\ningress {\n```\n\nStarts an inbound traffic rule.\n\n```hcl\nfrom_port = 443\nto_port   = 443\n```\n\nBoth values are 443, meaning the rule applies to that single destination port.\n\n```hcl\nprotocol = \"tcp\"\n```\n\nSpecifies TCP.\n\n```hcl\ncidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]\n```\n\nAllows matching traffic from all IPv4 addresses.\n\nThis is appropriate for a public HTTPS entry point when intended, but not for a private database.\n\n**Outbound rule**\n\n```hcl\negress {\n```\n\nDefines outgoing traffic.\n\n```hcl\nfrom_port = 0\nto_port   = 0\nprotocol  = \"-1\"\n```\n\nFor AWS security group rules, protocol `-1` represents all protocols. The port values do not restrict the protocol in this case.\n\n```hcl\ncidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]\n```\n\nAllows outbound IPv4 traffic to any destination.\n\nThis is a permissive outbound policy. Production systems may need more restrictive egress rules.\n\n### Validate the Terraform configuration\n\n```bash\nterraform init\nterraform validate\nterraform plan\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `terraform init` | Initializes the working directory and providers |\n| `terraform validate` | Checks configuration syntax and internal consistency |\n| `terraform plan` | Shows the changes Terraform intends to make |\n\nAfter reviewing the plan, a properly authorized operator can apply the changes:\n\n```bash\nterraform apply\n```\n\nReview the planned changes before approving them.\n\nDo not apply firewall configuration to a production environment without checking the impact on existing connectivity.\n\n## 12. Practical Exercise: Secure a Marketplace\n\nYou have been given three servers:\n\n```text\nFrontend: 10.0.1.10\nBackend:  10.0.2.10\nDatabase: 10.0.3.10\n```\n\nYour task is to design firewall rules.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Public users must access the frontend using HTTPS.\n2. The backend API must accept traffic only from the approved frontend entry point.\n3. MongoDB must accept connections only from the backend.\n4. SSH must be restricted to the administrator's trusted network.\n5. Unnecessary inbound traffic must be blocked.\n6. Outbound access must support the services' actual operational requirements.\n\n### Expected firewall policy\n\n| Resource | Port | Source |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Frontend | 443 | Public internet |\n| Backend | 3000 | Frontend or approved load balancer |\n| Database | 27017 | Backend |\n| All servers | 22 | Administrator network |\n\n### Your deliverables\n\nCreate:\n\n- A network architecture diagram.\n- An inbound security group rule table.\n- An outbound policy.\n- A list of unnecessary public ports to remove.\n- A troubleshooting procedure for failed API-to-database communication.\n\nExplain why each rule exists.\n\n## Part 3 Summary\n\nYou have practised:\n\n- Inspecting Linux network interfaces.\n- Inspecting listening services.\n- Configuring UFW.\n- Restricting MongoDB access.\n- Testing connectivity with Netcat.\n- Testing HTTP and HTTPS with curl.\n- Inspecting firewall logs.\n- Troubleshooting SSH and database connectivity.\n- Creating AWS security groups using Terraform.\n\n**Progress: Part 3 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 OF 4: KAI ASSESSMENT — CLOUD NETWORK SECURITY\n\nWelcome to Kai's assessment.\n\nYou have completed the foundational, advanced, and practical sections of this lesson.\n\nNow demonstrate that you can design, configure, and troubleshoot cloud network security.\n\n**Instructions:**\n\n- Answer every question.\n- Explain your reasoning where requested.\n- Write commands carefully.\n- For practical questions, explain what each command does.\n- Do not guess when you can reason from the network architecture.\n\n## SECTION A: MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n**1. What is the primary purpose of a firewall?**\n\nA. Increase CPU performance.  \nB. Control network traffic according to security rules.  \nC. Automatically encrypt every database.  \nD. Replace application authentication.\n\n**2. Which port is commonly used for HTTPS?**\n\nA. 22  \nB. 80  \nC. 443  \nD. 3306\n\n**3. Which protocol provides reliable, ordered data delivery?**\n\nA. UDP  \nB. TCP  \nC. ICMP  \nD. ARP\n\n**4. Which AWS service is a stateful virtual firewall associated with supported resources such as network interfaces?**\n\nA. Network ACL  \nB. Security Group  \nC. Route Table  \nD. Internet Gateway\n\n**5. Which AWS control is stateless and operates at the subnet level?**\n\nA. Security Group  \nB. Network ACL  \nC. IAM Role  \nD. NAT Gateway\n\n**6. What does `0.0.0.0/0` represent?**\n\nA. One IPv4 address.  \nB. A private IP address.  \nC. All IPv4 addresses.  \nD. A DNS server.\n\n**7. Which Linux utility provides a simplified firewall management interface?**\n\nA. UFW  \nB. Git  \nC. Docker  \nD. SSH\n\n**8. What does the principle of least privilege mean?**\n\nA. Give every service administrator access.  \nB. Allow all network traffic.  \nC. Provide only the access necessary for a task.  \nD. Disable all firewall rules.\n\n## SECTION B: SHORT-ANSWER QUESTIONS\n\n**9.** Define a firewall and explain its importance in cloud computing.\n\n**10.** Differentiate between inbound and outbound traffic. Give one example of each.\n\n**11.** Explain the difference between a security group and a traditional host-based firewall.\n\n**12.** What is the difference between stateful and stateless firewalls?\n\n**13.** Explain why a database server should not normally accept connections from the public internet.\n\n**14.** What is defence in depth? Explain how cloud security groups and UFW can work together.\n\n**15.** Explain why restricting SSH access to a trusted administrator network is better than allowing SSH from `0.0.0.0/0`.\n\n## SECTION C: PRACTICAL COMMAND QUESTIONS\n\n**16.** Write a Linux command to display the status of UFW.\n\n**17.** Write a UFW command that permits HTTPS traffic over TCP.\n\n**18.** Write a UFW command that permits SSH only from `198.51.100.25`.\n\n**19.** Write a command that displays listening TCP and UDP sockets with their associated processes.\n\n**20.** Write a command to test whether TCP port 27017 is reachable on `10.0.3.10`.\n\n**21.** Explain what the following command does:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw default deny incoming\n```\n\n**22.** Explain the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow from 10.0.2.10 to any port 27017 proto tcp\n```\n\n## SECTION D: TROUBLESHOOTING\n\n**23.** Your backend API cannot connect to MongoDB. List at least five checks you would perform before changing firewall rules.\n\n**24.** An administrator cannot SSH into a cloud server after enabling UFW. Explain the likely cause and how to prevent the problem.\n\n**25.** A developer opens TCP port 27017 to `0.0.0.0/0` to fix a database connection problem. Explain why this is dangerous and propose a safer configuration.\n\n**26.** A service is listening on `127.0.0.1:3000`, but another server cannot access it using the private IP address. Explain the problem.\n\n**27.** A connection to a server times out. Explain why this error alone cannot prove that the firewall is responsible.\n\n## SECTION E: CLOUD ARCHITECTURE CHALLENGE\n\n**28. Scenario:**\n\nYou are responsible for securing a production marketplace deployed in AWS.\n\nThe architecture contains:\n\n- A public load balancer.\n- Two application servers.\n- One MongoDB database server.\n- One background worker.\n\nDesign the security group rules.\n\nYour answer must include:\n\n1. The load balancer's inbound rules.\n2. The application servers' inbound rules.\n3. The database's inbound rules.\n4. The worker's required inbound and outbound access.\n5. SSH administration access.\n6. Which resources should not be publicly accessible.\n\n**29. Terraform challenge**\n\nExplain what each of these Terraform values means:\n\n```hcl\nfrom_port   = 443\nto_port     = 443\nprotocol    = \"tcp\"\ncidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]\n```\n\n**30. Security review**\n\nA production server has the following rules:\n\n| Port | Source | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| 22 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Allow |\n| 80 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Allow |\n| 443 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Allow |\n| 3306 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Allow |\n| 27017 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Allow |\n| 8080 | `0.0.0.0/0` | Allow |\n\nIdentify the security problems and propose a corrected rule set.\n\n---\n\n## FINAL PROJECT: SECURE A THREE-TIER CLOUD APPLICATION\n\nBuild a security design for a production marketplace.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```text\n                    INTERNET\n                        |\n                        v\n                 PUBLIC LOAD BALANCER\n                        |\n                        v\n                 APPLICATION SERVERS\n                        |\n               +--------+--------+\n               |                 |\n               v                 v\n           DATABASE         BACKGROUND WORKER\n```\n\n### Requirements\n\nYour design must include:\n\n| Area | Required work |\n|---|---|\n| Network access | Define inbound and outbound policies |\n| Public exposure | Identify publicly accessible resources |\n| Database security | Restrict database connections |\n| Administration | Secure SSH access |\n| Linux firewall | Demonstrate UFW configuration |\n| Cloud firewall | Define security group rules |\n| Troubleshooting | Explain how you would investigate blocked traffic |\n| Documentation | Provide a clear network security diagram |\n\n### Expected submission\n\nSubmit:\n\n1. A network diagram.\n2. A security group rule table.\n3. Linux firewall commands.\n4. An explanation of each security rule.\n5. A troubleshooting guide.\n6. A short security review identifying remaining risks.\n\n## HOW TO SUBMIT TO KAI\n\nSend Kai your answers using the following structure:\n\n```text\nCLOUD NETWORK SECURITY ASSESSMENT\n\nSection A:\n1. ...\n2. ...\n3. ...\n\nSection B:\n9. ...\n10. ...\n\nSection C:\n16. ...\n17. ...\n\nSection D:\n23. ...\n24. ...\n\nSection E:\n28. ...\n29. ...\n\nFinal Project:\n[Your architecture and security configuration]\n```\n\nKai should evaluate your answers, identify incorrect concepts, explain mistakes, and recommend areas that need more practice.\n\n## COMPLETION CHECKLIST\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to:\n\n- [ ] Explain the purpose of a firewall.\n- [ ] Differentiate security groups from host firewalls.\n- [ ] Explain inbound and outbound traffic.\n- [ ] Understand TCP and UDP firewall rules.\n- [ ] Explain stateful and stateless filtering.\n- [ ] Configure basic UFW rules.\n- [ ] Understand AWS Security Groups, Azure NSGs, and Google Cloud firewall rules.\n- [ ] Explain Network ACLs.\n- [ ] Restrict database access to trusted application resources.\n- [ ] Test network connectivity.\n- [ ] Troubleshoot firewall-related connection problems.\n- [ ] Understand firewall configuration using Terraform.\n- [ ] Design a secure cloud network.\n\n# LESSON COMPLETE\n\n**Part 4 of 4 — Lesson Complete**\n\n### Recommended next lesson\n\n`Cloud Networking Fundamentals: VPNs, Bastion Hosts and Private Connectivity`\n\nThis will introduce secure remote access, site-to-site VPNs, private communication between networks, bastion hosts, and cloud-to-cloud connectivity.";
